\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{R}{eal-time} load demand forecasting is vital for efficient and reliable electrical systems globally. \textcolor{black}{Accurate forecasts enable decision-making regarding dispatching generating units and scheduling transmission system maintenance and upgrades. \ac{VSTLF} predicts load behavior over a few minutes to several hours, widely used in operating electricity markets, including open and controlled environments \cite{Dannecker2015}. \ac{VSTLF} aims to predict the near-term behavior of electrical systems, supporting informed decision-making, including economic dispatch, price setting, renewable energy coordination, system analysis, and interchange scheduling \cite{Fan1994}.}


\textcolor{black}{Accurate load demand forecasting is crucial for maintaining power systems' economic viability and security.}
Deviations in the forecast that underestimate demand can result in additional costs associated with maintaining generator operation through ancillary services such as regulation and reserves. Conversely, overestimating demand can lead to unnecessary energy purchases and over-generation, compromising the delicate balance between generation and load and jeopardizing the system's security \cite{Yadav2023}. 

The challenge of estimating a \ac{VSTLF} involves finding a model that accurately predicts expected electricity demand and providing forecasts on time. The promptness of the forecast is of equal importance as its accuracy in scenarios where sudden changes in demand may occur, such as cascading failures or extreme weather events \cite{CascadeFailures}. The relationship between accuracy and calculation time remains unresolved, and efforts to improve one aspect often compromise the other. To mitigate this trade-off, solutions have been proposed that involve increasing computational capacity, processing costs, and energy consumption.

Therefore, in the context of a \ac{RTM}, system operators require efficient and reliable methods for \ac{VSTLF} that can automatically forecast load demand on a 24/7 basis, \textcolor{black}{providing results every five minutes and delivering them almost instantaneously.} Such models must meet the following criteria:
\begin{itemize}
    \item High accuracy: \textcolor{black}{It is crucial, as it directly impacts operational efficiency and the reliability of market results.} %The precision of the forecast is crucial as it affects the efficiency of operations and the validity of results published by the market.
    \item Speed: The model must provide results on time, ideally within ten seconds or less per series.
    \item Adaptability: \textcolor{black}{the model must adapt to changes in patterns and sudden shifts in load demand caused by external factors by integrating the most up-to-date real-time data.}  %The model must adjust to changes in patterns and sudden shifts in load demand due to external factors, incorporating the most recent real--time data to improve forecast accuracy.
    \item Low computational cost: \textcolor{black}{Essential when computing resources are limited and multiple forecasting methods are running on the same computer or shared with other processes.}  %This attribute is essential in scenarios where multiple forecasting methods are run on the same computer or shared with other processes, particularly when processing resources are limited.
    \item Robustness: \textcolor{black}{The model must consistently converge and provide accurate forecasts for the market, even in cases of non-invertible matrices and numerical inaccuracies during regression processes.}  %The model must demonstrate consistent convergence and provide accurate forecasts to the market; e.g., It should effectively handle situations of non--invertible matrices and numerical inaccuracies in regression processes if they occur.
    \item Low maintenance: The model parameters should require minimal manual intervention, and the number of tuning parameters should be kept to a minimum.
    \item Repeatability: The model should be deterministic, resulting in consistent results even when run multiple times. Retaining all input data is crucial for achieving reproducible results and obtaining approval from regulatory agencies during audits. %All input data must be retained to allow for the reproducibility of results, which is crucial for obtaining approval from regulatory agencies during audits.
    \end{itemize}
    
\textcolor{black}{A broad discussion of forecasting requirements for the electricity
markets is discussed by Dannecker \cite{Dannecker2015}.} %in which wee agree on some points.

\textcolor{black}{This paper's primary scientific contribution is the development of the \ac{AnMA} approach for predicting real-time load demand. By integrating the advantages of based-analogies (An) and moving averages (MA) methods, \ac{AnMA} offers a trustworthy and precise forecast that can rapidly respond to recent changes in the load. This innovative solution delivers a rare and coveted mix of speed, adaptability, and robustness, making it an ideal candidate for use in electricity market operations.}

\textcolor{black}{In addition, the proposed method design in this paper meets the specified requirements of \ac{RTM} forecasting by utilizing continuous retraining with incoming real-time data for immediate adaptation to changes in data behavior. It is specifically designed for very short-term load forecasting across seasons, each with distinctive demand patterns. Furthermore, the method optimizes training using a "first-in, last-out" approach that discards the latest training data when new real-time data is introduced.}

%The main contribution of this paper is the development of the \ac{AnMA} method for predicting real--time load demand. By combining the benefits of based-analogies (An) and moving averages (MA) methods, \ac{AnMA} provides a reliable and accurate forecast that can quickly respond to recent changes in the load. This innovative solution offers a rare and desirable combination of speed, adaptability, and robustness, making it an attractive choice for electricity market operations.

The five-minute and two-and-a-half hours test results demonstrated that the \ac{AnMA} variants using \ac{PCR}, Lasso, and Ridge were more precise and faster than the other methods.
\textcolor{black}{This study's proposal not only outlines a method but also suggests a comprehensive framework that integrates various correlation and regression tools from both classical statistics and \ac{ML}. This sets a benchmark for future research aiming to improve load demand forecasting methods' efficiency.}

The paper is structured as follows: Section \ref{sec:RelatedWork} \textcolor{black}{presents a thorough overview of previous research on \ac{VSTLF}, and works related to the analogue method in other fields are present.} Section \ref{sec:MaterialsandMethods} outlines the proposed \ac{AnMA} method. The test procedures are explained in section \ref{sec:Validation}. The main test results are summarized in section \ref{sec:Results}. \textcolor{black}{Section \ref{sec:Discussion} discusses the key findings of the tests.} Finally, Section \ref{sec:Conclusion} summarizes all the research and outlines potential lines for future research.

\section{Related work}
\label{sec:RelatedWork}
Load demand forecasting has been the subject of extensive research \cite{Nti2020,Abdul2021}. This section provides an overview of some of the most relevant studies, including those based on \ac{ML} techniques such as \ac{ANN}  \cite{Baric2019,Gunawan2021} and \ac{SVR}. 

A noteworthy study is documented by Capuno et al. \cite{Capuno2017}, who presented a method for a real-time operation that employs a hybrid of algebraic prediction and \ac{SVR}. Their method initially generates a first forecast and then employs \ac{SVR} to rectify any variations caused by significant temperature and humidity changes. The proposed method in our work also generates a baseline forecast and adjusts it with real-time data.

\textcolor{black}{ \ac{ML} algorithms can deliver high-accuracy results but require high computational cost during the training phase \cite{Ibrahim2022,Yadav2023,Makridakis2020}. In addition, changes in load patterns may necessitate frequent retraining of ML models, leading to increased resource consumption and a carbon footprint \cite{shamikEA2021}. More sophisticated methods, such as those incorporating \ac{DL} models, have been proposed for load demand forecasting  \cite{Tong2022,Ribeiro2022}. However, these methods face lengthy training times, further exacerbated with each retraining due to adding new demand data. Therefore, statistical learning should balance accuracy and computational efficiency, reducing resource usage and the carbon footprint as much as possible. On the contrary, linear regression-based methods, which are computationally inexpensive but may not be as accurate due to their linear features, frequently employ environmental factors as regression variables to model electricity demand \cite{Hannah2019}. }

The Analogies method, initially used in meteorology and climatology, has been proposed as another approach to time series forecasting by Monache et al. \cite{Monache2013},
This method assumes that forecasting errors between similar days will likely occur again.
The grouping of days is performed by calculating the distance between all the days to select the most similar, resulting in an analog space consisting of days with similar features and subsequent error data. 
This model has been applied successfully in forecasting renewable resources in photovoltaic plants and wind farms by Alessandrini et al. \cite{Alessandrini2014, Alessandrini2015}. 
This approach makes a forecast from a set of the most similar past predictions.
Azevedo et al. \cite{CostaEA2021} and Santis et al. \cite{Santis2023} describe its dynamic time scan forecasting based on analogies as a fast method for large datasets to forecast wind speed time series. The scan procedure uses polynomial regression models as a distance measure to identify similar patterns throughout the time series. The author's innovative idea was to use different similarity metrics as variables in the An method.
%Instead, \textcolor{black}{the Pearson correlation coefficient is selected as a similarity metric due to its fast calculation speed.} Additionally, a final correction in real-time is made.

The multi-seasonal characteristics of electricity demand
have been thoroughly studied by Gould et al. \cite{Gould2008} and Livera et al. \cite{Alysha2011}, 
who have identified multiple seasonalities in the time series.
These seasonalities define demand patterns between days and seasons.
Dudek \cite{Dudek2015a} conducted a study on the seasonal characteristics of demand and determined certain patterns using tools such as \ac{NN}. He found the criteria between days of the same season by minimizing the distances between a window of the latest data and the time series.

In a follow-up work, Dudek \cite{Dudek2016} proposes a one-day-ahead
load demand forecasting model based on the search for patterns using
\ac{NN} and combining sample selection with \ac{OLS}. 
This model refines the regression using variable reduction techniques such as stepwise regression, Lasso, and \ac{PCR}. 
The study's central aim is to accurately identify days with seasonal cycle patterns and classify these days into weekdays, Saturdays, Sundays, and holidays. %\textcolor{red}{Finally, the model is applied for forecasting the day ahead.}
The ideas presented by Dudek \cite{Dudek2016} are comparable to those in our proposed method, including the selection of samples, the regression of these samples to determine a model, and the forecast calculation based on the subsequent data from the samples.

The work by Ngo et al. \cite{VietCuong2015} proposes a method for ultra-short-term load demand forecasting based on the robust Holt-Winters double seasonal model. The authors evaluated the performance of the proposed method on distribution feeder loads and found that it had good prediction accuracy. \ac{ETS} methods are widely recognized for their efficiency, performance, and low computational cost and are commonly used as a benchmark in forecasting.

\textcolor{black}{A novel approach for \ac{VSTLF} is presented in our study, which combines an analogue approach with a moving averages model. This is the first time that such a combination of similarity metrics has been reported in a unified framework, to the best of our knowledge. A significant contribution to the field has been made by utilizing regression models, selecting high-correlation days, and correcting baseline forecast errors in real-time. The novel methodology has the potential to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of \ac{VSTLF} in the electricity markets.}















