\subsection*{Stage iii. An residuals estimation}
\label{ma_method}
The An method identifies repeating patterns in the data and uses those patterns to make a baseline forecast. However, over time, this baseline forecast may become biased and susceptible to errors if there are sudden changes in the demand data. To avoid this, making real-time adjustments while the forecast is being calculated is necessary.

%The \textcolor{black}{An} method creates a series of errors between the forecast ($Y'$) and the actual demand ($S$). 
\textcolor{black}{From the baseline forecast results obtained by the An method, a series of errors between $Y'$ and the actual demand $S$ is created.}
The error series is referred to as $\epsilon={\epsilon_t:t \in [-v,0]}$ and contains the most recent $v$ errors of the An method. The error series is important because it corrects the new forecasts being calculated by An. It is essential to keep the results of the baseline forecast obtained using the An method, as they will be used to make these corrections.

\subsection*{Stage iv. MA estimation}
\label{ma_estimation_method}
\textcolor{black}{In the fourth stage, a model MA of order $q$ is set from the errors ($\epsilon$) of An to calculate the baseline forecast errors $\hat{\epsilon}$}.
%\textcolor{black}{The fourth stage involves the determination of MA from $\epsilon$ to compute the following baseline forecast errors: $\hat{\epsilon}$}.

\subsection*{Stage v. AnMA forecast}
\label{anma_forecast}

Finally, the An and MA composite method computes the final forecast, denoted as $Y''$, by adding the calculated $\hat{\epsilon}$ to the result of the An forecast $Y'$, as expressed by the equation 
\begin{equation}
Y''= Y'(t) + \hat{\epsilon}(t), \ \forall t={1,\ldots,w}.
\end{equation}






\subsection{AnMA variants}

The \ac{AnMA} methodology initially used purely statistical tools but later incorporated tools from the statistical and machine learning fields in neighbor selection and regression. \textcolor{black}{The basic version of AnMA uses the Pearson coefficient for distance metric and {OLS} for regression. However, } the first variation altered the neighbor distance measure from Pearson correlation to Euclidean distance in the selection phase. The second variation used linear dimensional reduction methods such as \ac{PCR} and \ac{PLS} and shrinkage techniques like Lasso and Ridge regression to minimize the penalized residual sum of squares in the regression phase. Other models used include \ac{RF}, Bagging, and Boosting, which employ the concept of ensembles to produce more accurate predictions.
\textcolor{black}{Tests of the \ac{AnMA} method were conducted using the combinations of these tools.} %, with only the best results presented in this work.













\subsection{Method Validation}
\label{sec:Validation}
Two tests will be conducted. The first will compare five-minute forecasts using \ac{AnMA} and variants to \ac{HWA}, \ac{HWM}, and Naïve models. The second test (prefix \texttt{X}) will compare two-and-a-half-hour forecasts with thirty periods in five-minute intervals using \ac{AnMA} and variants, \ac{HWA}, \ac{HWM}, and \ac{ARMA}, based on accuracy, calculation time, and CPU usage. A subsequence size of 288 data points will be used for $v_1$, with \ac{AnMA} variants using five neighbors and MA using 15 periods for baseline forecast error correction. \ac{AnMA} variants will use \ac{PLS}, \ac{PCR}, Lasso, Ridge, \ac{RF}, Bagging, and Boosting regression models with two different neighbor selection metrics: Pearson's coefficient (default) and Euclidean distance. Some variants will omit the MA correction, indicated by the prefix \texttt{An}. %(suffix \texttt{euclidean})

Auto-ARIMA was attempted \cite{Hyndman2008,garza2022statsforecast}, but %
it was unfeasible due to the high processing time \textcolor{black}{per real-time}
forecast. Instead, a faster 7-day lag \ac{ARMA} ($288 \times 7$ periods) to capture daily/weekly electricity demand seasonality is used 
\cite{Alysha2011} and implement an error correction using $15$ periods lag MA model. For validation in time series
forecasting, $k$-fold cross-validation will be used, following
Bergmeir et al. \cite{Bergmeir2018}. 
\textcolor{black}{All methods were tested with a single processing core except for ARMA, which utilized the \texttt{statsmodel} \cite{seabold2010statsmodels} library to exploit all available computer resources for faster processing.}

The forecast will be for the summer of 2010, using four months of training data to predict the next 30 data periods. When new real-time data is added, the last training data will be trimmed with a first-in, last-out approach. 

The accuracy metrics will be expressed in the \ac{MAPE} and \ac{MAE}. The computation time will be measured in seconds. And finally, the usage of CPU in percentage, which is recorded on a minute-by-minute basis.

\textcolor{black}{A factorial design of experiments is recommended to explore the impact of varying the number of neighbors $k$, subsequence size $v_1$, and the $q$ order or MA. The optimal parameter values were determined to be $k=10$, $v_1=288$, and MA=15. Specifically, these parameter values correspond to a number of neighbors equivalent to ten different days, a subsequence length of one day, and an MA order of one- and-a-half-hours.}










